**EXERCISE 5.13.** Prove the existence of a one-one function $\mathrm{num} : \mathbf{N} \times \mathbf{N} \to \mathbf{N}$ such that

$$
\mathrm{num}(0, 0) = 0;
$$

$$
\mathrm{num}(n, m+1) = \mathrm{num}(n+1, m) + 1;
$$

$$
\mathrm{num}(n+1, 0) = \mathrm{num}(0, n) + 1.
$$

Draw a picture (i.e. an infinite matrix) for the function and find a closed form for its values, if possible. Use the function to prove the isomorphism of the domains

$$
P\mathbf{N},\ P(\mathbf{N} \times \mathbf{N}),\ P\mathbf{N} \times P\mathbf{N}.
$$

<!-- page 90 -->

The pairing is with the
[natural-number pairing](1980_natPair.md) peer; the powerset isomorphisms
are with the
[powerset isomorphisms](1980_powersetIso.md) peer.

<!-- formalization -->

**Theorem (`example_513b`).** There are order isomorphisms among the power set of the naturals, the power set of pairs of naturals, and the product of two power sets of naturals.

*Proof.* The three order isomorphisms from the powerset-isomorphisms peer. ∎
